+## Replies
+
+### A, NOAA bottlenose dolphins: first reply, 23 Nov 2026
+
+**How it reached this record.** Chad pasted the email thread into the Claude thread on 24 Nov 2026 as an update for the Animals thread. Claude has not seen the inbox. The paste showed the reply as "Mon, Nov 23, 10:22 PM (6 hours ago)", addressed to Chad and three other people shown by first name only; the time zone is not stated in the paste. Subject as pasted: "Re: Correct contact for bottlenose dolphin telemetry data — InPort 26479". Phone numbers and email addresses in the paste are left out of this public repository; Chad holds them. Chad had also sent the reply to GPT; GPT's summary, relayed by Chad, reads it the same way as below (they ask to narrow the request; no data access or reuse permission granted yet).
+
+**The reply, as pasted, from Jenny Litz, Ph.D. (Marine Mammal Branch Chief, Southeast Fisheries Science Center, Miami, Florida; NOAA Fisheries, U.S. Department of Commerce):**
+
+> Hi Chad -
+>
+> Thank you for reaching out.  We are looking into this for you.  Can you please let us know if you are specifically looking for animal tracks along the mid-Atlantic coast or are tracks from tagged animals in the Gulf of america also acceptable for your purposes?  Are you looking to highlight the journey of just a few animals?
+>
+> Thanks.  We will get back to you as soon as possible but likely after the Thanksgiving holiday.
+>
+> Jenny
+
+**What it is, and is not (Claude's reading).**
+- It is a reply from NOAA's Southeast Fisheries Science Center. They are looking into the request and ask two questions: whether tracks from tagged animals in the Gulf are acceptable as well as tracks along the mid-Atlantic coast, and whether the aim is the journey of just a few animals.
+- They expect to come back after the Thanksgiving holiday (26 Nov 2026 in the United States).
+- It answers none of the enquiry's questions (how the data can be obtained, processing, hosting and display, redistribution, how positions were obtained, location protections, credit).
+- **It is not data access and it is not permission.** Nothing about a bottlenose dolphin journey is cleared by it. Any dataset offered would be a new study with its own source audit before any proposal.
+- Answering her two questions is Chad's. Claude sends nothing. No follow-up beyond his answer.
+- Chad's rule of 23 Nov 2026 applies to his answer: it makes clear that Mother Earth is his own personal control centre, not for public use, and says in the same breath that the page and the repository are reachable on the web by anyone with the link. The enquiry already out described the site as a website presenting dated observations with their sources; nothing is resent.
+
+**The covering note as sent (quoted in the reply; Chad's sign-off kept, his phone number and the lines after it left out, the bounced address left out):**
+
+> Hello Jenny,
+>
+> I’m trying to reach the current contact for NOAA’s “Bottlenose dolphin satellite telemetry spreadsheets (many)” dataset, InPort item 26479:
+>
+> https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/inport/item/26479
+>
+> The record lists Aleta Hohn, but my email to [address left out] was rejected with “550 5.7.1 unrecognized address”.
+>
+> I run Mother Earth, a website presenting dated observations with their sources:
+>
+> https://theonetheonlythechad.github.io/mother-earth/
+>
+> I’d like to explore using a small, completed dolphin telemetry study for a clearly labelled historical journey replay, with appropriate attribution and any necessary location protections.
+>
+> Could you please forward my enquiry to whoever now manages these data, or suggest the correct contact? I’ve included my original enquiry below for context.
+>
+> Ta
+> Chad
+
+The original enquiry that followed the note was not shown in the paste.
